**Pass.** This artifact is readable and actionable without access to the original ChatGPT thread, project, or either PDF. All retained signal is restated as TF-specific proposals with concrete field lists, not as pointers back to the source.

## Source synopsis

The user pasted a multi-turn ChatGPT conversation from a custom GPT project dedicated to their "Magnus Progenitor Saga" (MPS) property and its meta-narrative companion "Biases as Constants" (BAC). The conversation covers three distinct asks. First, a plan to recover book-by-book ~500-word synopses (goal-oriented, not plot summaries) for MPS's seven novel volumes plus the BAC companion codex, in a fixed working order. Second, in response to "am I over-tooled," a full inventory-and-role-assignment strategy across ten tools (ChatGPT, Claude, Notion, GitHub, Replit, GitHub Copilot, Mermaid.ai, Perplexity, Microsoft 365, Gemini) plus four hardware devices, built around the idea that "every tool gets a job, no tool gets the whole kingdom." That turn recommends a Notion-centric content-operations model: one Notion HQ with nine core databases (Works, Chapters/Sections, Sources/Imports, Canon Entities, Canon Terms/Glossary, Motifs/Themes, Timeline Events, Revision Runs/Maturity, AI Council Outputs) feeding a single private GitHub repo (`magnus-progenitor-system`) as the canonical archive, with a documented Markdown frontmatter standard bridging the two and a phased migration sequence. Third, a domain-name and release-title strategy question — whether the title "The Magnus Progenitor Saga" is strong enough to commit to, and whether to register defensive Dynadot domains now versus building a standalone site.

