## Decisions and rationale

### 1. Tool-role specialization — corroboration only, no new decision

The thread's "AI Council" model (each AI/tool gets one job) is the same instinct behind TF's confirmed three-tier ingestion architecture and the PME/PIE/CME/CIE engine-family split. This is `stated` corroborating evidence from an independent context, not new design input. No action item.

### 2. Notion-centric content-ops architecture — reaffirmed against, not reopened

The thread's central recommendation is a Notion HQ (9 databases) as the primary "living workspace," with GitHub as a secondary durable archive fed *from* Notion. Project memory records at least three other MPS/BAC-sourced threads independently proposing a Notion-centered architecture before this one (`[[telling_forward_gemini_thread_context]]`, the BAC manuscript pass in `[[telling_forward_bac_manuscript_adr]]`, and the clarity-pass thread behind ADR-0007's "Notion canon-hub additions"), making this at least the fourth. TF's 2026-08-17 confirmed decision — GitHub-only, no Notion dependency, capsules as GitHub Issues — stands. Nothing here reopens it; it's one more data point that TF's call diverges from this property's own operating pattern, not evidence against TF's choice.

### 3. Frontmatter YAML standard — reusable pattern, informs capsule/scene markdown design

The thread's proposed frontmatter for exported chapters (`id`, `work_id`, `section_type`, `order`, `canon_layer`, `status`, `version`, `source_document`, `characters`, `motifs`, `canon_terms`, `github_path`) is a concrete, directly transferable pattern for TF's own markdown-native scene/capsule files (confirmed format per `[[telling_forward_ui_vision]]`). This is a `proposal`-tier signal — no TF file currently defines a frontmatter schema for scene markdown.

### 4. Canon Terms/Glossary database — concretizes ADR-0006 item 2

The thread's "Canon Terms" schema (Term, Definition, Tier: Plain/Symbolic/Mythic/System/Forbidden-Deprecated, Casing Rule, Canon Status, First Appearance, Drift Risk, Related Entities, Related Motifs) is a field-level precedent for ADR-0006 item 2's "steward-facing term/motif ledger," which is currently an unspecified proposal blocked on the `capsules` table. This gives that proposal a starting field list rather than a blank page.

### 5. Revision Runs/Maturity + AI Council Outputs databases — concretize ADR-0005 item 2

The thread's "Revision Runs" schema (Run Type, Maturity Before/After using an R0–R10 rung scale, Issue Type: Drift/Tone/Continuity/Glossary/Prose/Canon/Structure, AI Tool Used, Accepted?, GitHub Commit) and "AI Council Outputs" schema (Tool, Output Type, Quality Rating, Use Decision: Accepted/Partial/Rejected/Reference) are field-level precedents for ADR-0005 item 2's "side-car provenance layer" — which is currently defined only as "which tier or engine produced or matured it." The R0–R10 rung scale in the thread's schema is the same PME maturity ladder TF has already confirmed as inherited lineage, used here as an actual database column rather than a described concept — direct evidence the rung value belongs in a provenance record as structured data, not prose.

### 6. "Canon as Code" — an unnamed principle TF already practices (added on extension pass)

The thread's own framing of Biases as Constants — canon as "executable," memory as "compression protocol," structure over recall — is the same underlying justification TF already acted on when it chose GitHub Issues over prose files for capsules (per `[[telling_forward_ui_vision]]`'s "why capsules are GitHub Issues, not prose files" note), but no TF document states the principle itself, only its one applied instance. Naming it gives future schema calls (including items 4 and 5 above) a citable rationale instead of re-deriving it each time.

### 7. Immutable raw-source rule for manuscript ingestion (added on extension pass)

The thread's migration plan opens with "freeze the source" — raw imports archived, nothing in that archive ever edited, treated as evidence. ADR-0004's ingestion pipeline transforms an uploaded manuscript through three tiers but never states whether the original upload is preserved unmodified once ingestion starts. This is a gap, not a new idea; principle #4 ("keep provenance visible") already implies it.

### 8. Repo-splitting heuristic for platform code (added on extension pass)

The thread's own repo-consolidation reasoning — split a repo only when a component's lifecycle genuinely diverges, not by content category — is a reusable decision test independent of its Notion-centric context. TF has already settled content-repo topology (one repo per storyworld); this heuristic is for a different, still-open question: how the Author App, Reader App, ingestion scripts, and MCP server split (or don't) across repos.

